It was set up in **1999** under the IRDA Act, 1999, and received formal statutory status on **19 April 2000**. It regulates, promotes, and ensures orderly growth of the insurance and reinsurance industries in India.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It was initially set up in New Delhi in 1999-2000 and was shifted to Hyderabad in **2001**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Its core purpose is to establish an independent statutory authority to **regulate and develop** the insurance industry in India, protect policyholders'' interests, and end the public-sector monopoly that existed before liberalisation.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is fixed at the time of buying the policy and does not depend on investment performance (unlike the fund value in a ULIP).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is calculated based on factors like age, sum assured, policy term, health, and the type of plan.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is lower than the total premiums paid, since the insurer deducts charges for providing cover and administration.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Common riders include the **accidental death benefit rider**, **critical illness rider**, **waiver of premium rider**, and **term rider**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is governed by **Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938**. A nominee only receives the money on behalf of the legal heirs unless they are also a beneficial nominee (close family member).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Assignment (Section 38)** transfers the ownership rights of the policy itself to another person, who then becomes the policyholder. An assignment, once validly made, automatically cancels an earlier nomination unless it is made in the insurer''s favour as security for a loan.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It has **no maturity benefit** and no savings component, which is why its premium is much lower than other plans for the same sum assured.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It combines a savings/investment component with life cover and pays the sum assured plus bonuses either on death or on reaching the specified maturity age.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is more expensive than term insurance because part of the premium goes towards building a maturity benefit.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It combines **life insurance with market-linked investment**: part of the premium goes towards mortality (insurance) charges and part is invested in equity, debt, or balanced funds chosen by the policyholder, with the fund value fluctuating with market performance.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

The balance sum assured, along with bonuses, is paid at maturity or on death, with the full sum assured payable on death regardless of survival benefits already paid.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Annuities can be **immediate** (payouts start right away) or **deferred** (payouts start after an accumulation period).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

As per IRDAI norms, it is typically **15 days** for monthly premium policies and **30 days** for other modes (quarterly, half-yearly, yearly).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A lapsed policy can usually be **revived** within a specified revival period (commonly up to five years) by paying overdue premiums with interest, subject to the insurer''s underwriting requirements.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

IRDAI mandates a free-look period of **15 days** from the date of receipt of the policy document, extended to **30 days** for policies sold through distance marketing/electronic mode.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Common types include **reversionary bonus** (added annually and payable at maturity/death) and **terminal bonus** (a one-time bonus paid at maturity or claim).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is fundamental because, without it, insurance would amount to a **wagering contract**, which is legally unenforceable.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Failure to disclose material information (**non-disclosure** or **misrepresentation**) can make the policy voidable by the insurer, unlike ordinary contracts which only require good faith, not utmost good faith.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This principle applies to general insurance (fire, marine, motor) but **not to life insurance**, since human life cannot be given a precise monetary value.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It prevents the insured from recovering twice for the same loss (once from the insurer and again from the negligent third party) and applies mainly to contracts of indemnity.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It prevents the insured from making a profit by claiming the full loss amount from each insurer separately.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

